  {
   "k": "1lt40x4",
   "en": "In 1921, the Tuvan People's Revolutionary Party, backed by the Red Army, took control of Tuva, installing Mongush Nimachap and Mongush Buyan-Badyrgy as leaders of the newly formed Tuvan People's Republic. In 1925, former monk Donduk Kuular became Prime Minister of ⟦0⟧, improving relations with Mongolia and declaring Buddhism the state religion. In 1929, after four years in power, Joseph Stalin orchestrated a coup by sending Tuvan youths to depose Kuular and bring ⟦1⟧ into closer alignment with the ⟦2⟧. Several short-lived leaders followed until Salchak Toka assumed control in 1932. Toka suppressed religion in Tuva and initiated rapid modernization, including the industrialization of the urban center of Kyzyl.",
